  Healers have the best possible ranged weapons.  They're unlimited
range, self targeting, don't required a wielded launcher, can't get
cursed, don't raise your experience level, and don't take up a turn to
use: Pets.

  Convert rocks to meatballs with the spell of stone to flesh for
taming, training, and keeping pets close.  You can keep your pets and
yourself fed with stone to fleshed boulders.

  Tame every horse, dog, and cat.  Taming them also keeps your other
pets from killing them.  Getting too many pets is self-correcting.
Better yet, leave some behind as a stash of replacements.

  When were-foo'd, use #monster to summon an army of your own.

<http://groups.google.com/group/rec.games.roguelike.nethack/msg/43c2571fcfb5f6b2?hl=en>

  There's a reason Evil Overlords have armies of minions.  

  The best item a healer can find early is a magic whistle.  "Meat
shields to me!"


  Three large cats/dogs will just clean out Mine levels.  You can
safely explore while they play with their food.

  Casting healing in combat is chancy, as you are learning.  Better
not to take the damage in the first place:  Run away!  Run away!
And wear the best armor you can find that doesn't burden you.  If you
do start to take damage, then retreat to a safe place to remove armor
and cast the healing spells.  

  You *can* have it all if you're willing to find a safe place to
remove armor temporarily.  Remember too that large shields can be
taken off in a single turn, spell cast, then worn in another single
turn.  Ditto for helmets and gloves.

  Once you accept that armor and spell casting can be alternated, try
on every non-cursed piece of armor you find.  Get some +4 pieces and
you're in a lot better shape.

  Even the most power mad spellcaster (i.e. me) spends less than 1% of
turns casting spells.  The rest of the time you might as well be
wearing the best armor you can find.  Learn to cast spells offline.

  For healer ranged missile weapons, darts aren't bad.  They can be
poisoned.  A healers are poison resistant so can untrap dart traps
without risk of poison instadeath.


>There's got to be a trick to this.

  NetHack isn't one trick.  NetHack is a thousand tricks.

  There are a hundred tricks for healers, from forcing pets down a
corridor ahead of you as a meat shield, to kicking at a monster
blocking a corridor so that it jumps to the other side of you (Yes,
really!), to flinging missiles down a dark corridor as reconnaissance
by fire.

  Here's a post I made on early healers.  A lot of the info can be
applied to the protection racket.
<http://groups.google.com/group/rec.games.roguelike.nethack/browse_thread/thread/b9b6bee57a7fe3de/b2f705f44328c6d4?hl=en#b2f705f44328c6d4>

  All the credit goes to tricks I picked up here in RGRN.

  Once you start leveling up, practice and throw daggers as a good
ranged weapon.  Or stay in character by poisoning and throwing darts
(Healers stick needles into people.  Get it?)  Once you get the
luckstone and max luck, bless your darts so they'll last.  Be on the
lookout for Tourist bones! (Another great trick.)

  That instadeath chance is great if you throw early and often.  I
don't recommend #enhancing those much if at all, but there's a whole
other encyclopedia to be written about Healer skill #enhancement
choices.




